From a43610e7d613a2bfefdbebe103055e1421d84751 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 20:42:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] patches: make the series generator implement its own
 convergence rule

series check --ref master exited 1 on master from two independent causes. Both are fixed here; neither was caused by any topic branch.

CAUSE 1 - the generator never implemented the exclusion its own commit message documents. 5d1486012 states it plainly: 'The generated series excludes patches/series/ so that the artifact converges (otherwise regenerating emits a patch containing the patch files, committing that changes the series again, and there is no fixpoint).' arifi_sync.py format_patch() had no such exclusion, so it regenerated 43 patches against the 40 that were committed - and the committed 40 are EXACTLY what the exclusion rule produces, i.e. the artifact was right and the checker was wrong. The three commits the rule drops (4e7c13ce3, e78d7ed50, 279f59f11) change nothing outside the generated directory.

The fixpoint the tool asked for was unreachable, not merely unreached: the commit that publishes a series can never be inside the series it published, because format-patch writes the commit's own sha into its From line, so a self-containing patch cannot exist. Adding ':(exclude)patches/series' makes a series-only commit generate nothing, which is what makes the second regeneration produce zero diff.

Attribution is now read from each patch's own 'From <sha>' header instead of zipping the file list against rev-list. The zip was order-dependent and could not survive a generator that legitimately drops commits: it would have mis-attributed every row after the first drop while still passing its own length check.

The replay check now diffs the replayed tree against the reference outside patches/series rather than comparing whole-tree hashes, so it still proves the git-am promise exactly.

CAUSE 2 - core.autocrlf=true (the Git-for-Windows install default) checked the committed patches out as CRLF while format-patch writes LF, so the byte-compare could never pass on any Windows clone. Patch 0001 measured 61570 bytes with 1132 CR against a freshly generated 60438 with 0, byte-equal after normalisation. Fixed in .gitattributes rather than in the comparison, so the bytes on disk are correct for every future clone instead of the difference being hidden at compare time: 'patches/** -text diff'. -text was chosen over 'text eol=lf' because eol=lf would strip payload CR bytes on check-in - 0007 legitimately carries 3 - and corrupt the patch. 'diff' keeps the files reviewable as text.

Stale manifest text corrected while regenerating it: the binary-payload patch number is now read off the artifact instead of hardcoded 0008 (it is 0007), the patch count is no longer hardcoded 38, and the banked PrismML patches are cited at patches/banked-source/ where 5d1486012 moved them.

Taken-from: ArifiLabs lane-110C fork-maintainability slot

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110C

Measured-effect: tooling and generated-artifact rules only; no engine behaviour changed. series check --ref master goes 1 -> 0; 40/40 patches byte-identical to a fresh generation and git am replays them to a tree identical to master outside patches/series.
